# Experiment 01: LED Blinking Using Arduino UNO Pin 1

## Board
Arduino UNO R3

## Pin Used
Digital Pin 13 (Built-in LED – L)

## Components Required
- Arduino UNO R3
- USB Cable
- Computer with Arduino IDE

## Working Principle
Pin 13 is configured as an output. The Arduino sends HIGH and LOW signals alternately to control the onboard LED.

## Arduino Code


void setup() {
  pinMode(13, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  digitalWrite(13, HIGH);
  delay(1000);

  digitalWrite(13, LOW);
  delay(1000);
}

## Code Explanation
- pinMode(13, OUTPUT) – Configures digital pin 13 as an output.
- digitalWrite(13, HIGH) – Turns ON the built-in LED.
- delay(1000) – Waits for 1 second.
- digitalWrite(13, LOW) – Turns OFF the built-in LED.
- loop() – Repeats the operation continuously.

## Working Sequence
1. Arduino initializes digital pin 13 as OUTPUT.
2. LED turns ON.
3. Arduino waits for 1 second.
4. LED turns OFF.
5. Arduino waits for 1 second.
6. The sequence repeats continuously.

## ON Time
1 second (1000 milliseconds)

## OFF Time
1 second (1000 milliseconds)

## Expected Result
The onboard LED marked L on the Arduino UNO blinks continuously at one-second intervals.






